QA = [
 ("What is distinctive about the proposal?", "It converts the installed factory-CNG base rather than building new vehicles, keeps petrol as an automatic backup so no car is stranded by missing stations, and, unlike most hydrogen pitches, states the price at which it works (₹188/kg delivered for CNG fuel parity) and the gates at which it stops."),
 ("Why retrofit this vehicle?", "The Dzire Tour S CNG is the fleet workhorse with a 4-cylinder 1.2 L engine (power margin after the hydrogen derate), a boot bay that already carries a 55 L cylinder, hardened valve seats, a 37 L petrol tank for backup, and the largest donor pool (Maruti sells about 70 percent of India's CNG cars)."),
 ("Why would the customer choose it over staying on CNG or buying a BEV?", f"On money alone they would not: continuing on CNG costs ₹{S['lcA']:.1f}/km, a compact BEV ₹{S['lcC']:.1f}/km including purchase, and the retrofit ₹{S['lcB']:.1f}/km at 2028 hydrogen prices. The customer for the pilot is a fleet with a decarbonisation mandate or a public programme paying for abatement and data; the commercial customer appears only if delivered hydrogen falls below about ₹190/kg."),
 ("Does the hydrogen storage system actually fit?", "A 60 L composite cylinder (OD 400 mm, 767 mm long, 36 kg) fits the transverse bay used by the 55 L CNG cylinder (OD 316 mm, about 900 mm), but it is 84 mm larger in diameter, so the boot loses a further 40 to 60 L. No under-floor option exists at 163 mm ground clearance. Phase 0 includes a physical mock-up because this is a concept, not validated design work."),
 ("What is sacrificed in passenger capacity, luggage or payload?", f"Seating is unchanged (five). Luggage falls from roughly 200 L with CNG to about 150 L. Kerb mass falls by about {abs(E['netmass']):.0f} kg, so payload is unchanged or slightly better. Peak power in hydrogen mode is about {E['h2ps']:.0f} PS, 48 percent below petrol; the car switches to petrol automatically for high load."),
 ("Which components cannot be reused?", "Every CNG fuel-system part: the steel cylinder (ISO 11439 steels are limited to 2 percent hydrogen), regulator, rail, injectors, hoses and lines. Hydrogen parts need UN R134 or ISO 19881 class qualification. The engine, gearbox, petrol system, catalyst and body are retained."),
 ("What is the credible pilot conversion cost?", f"₹{L(B['pilot'])} lakh per car including GST, dominated by the imported cylinder and valve at small quantities; ₹{L(B['volume'])} lakh installed at 10,000 kits per year (₹2.3 lakh ex-works). Round 1's ₹1.8 to 2.2 lakh is roughly the ex-works volume figure without taxes and margin."),
 ("Where will hydrogen come from and at what delivered price?", f"Certified green hydrogen by tube trailer from a SIGHT-awarded producer to a shared dispensing point in Faridabad. Our build-up gives about ₹{bd['delivered'][0]:,.0f}/kg at the pilot point (150 kg/day, 50 percent used) and about ₹{bd['delivered'][1]:,.0f}/kg at a 2030 hub (500 kg/day, 70 percent used); CEEW's central 2030 estimate is ₹360/kg. India's record refinery-gate tender is ₹279/kg before compression and dispensing."),
 ("What if fuel cost stays higher than expected?", "The pilot still delivers its purpose (approval route, real-world data, supplier readiness) because fuel is a programme cost. The scale decision at gate 4 simply fails, and the programme stops and publishes."),
 ("What if the station is unavailable?", "Every car runs on petrol automatically (555 km on a tank); the fleet loses the hydrogen benefit, not the service. The station partner carries utilisation risk through a take-or-pay contract, and a 1.5-day cascade buffer covers delivery slips."),
 ("What is the exact approval uncertainty?", "There is no standard or rule for a hydrogen retrofit kit. AIS-195 covers new hydrogen ICE vehicles; AIS-157 (fuel cell) explicitly excludes retrofits; Rule 126 and Rule 115 provide the retrofit machinery only for CNG and LPG. We need either an AIS-195 extension or a Rule 115 hydrogen-kit provision; the trial itself can run under a trade certificate with test-agency evaluation, as the Mirai and Tata trials did."),
 ("How is NOx addressed?", "Lean burn above lambda 2 gives near-zero engine-out NOx at steady state; transients do not stay lean, so the kit carries a lean NOx trap provision and lambda control, and the gate-2 test requires 60 mg/km or better on the BS-VI cycle. This is a target to demonstrate."),
 ("What is the environmental benefit under matched assumptions?", f"With certified green hydrogen (2 kg CO2e/kg) the converted car emits about {EM['Bgreen']:.0f} g CO2e/km well-to-wheel including the kit, against {EM['A']:.0f} on CNG and {EM['C30']:.0f} for a new BEV on the 2030 grid including its manufacturing. With grid-electrolysis hydrogen it would be four times worse than CNG, so the fuel's certification is a condition, not a detail. Hydrogen combustion also emits NOx."),
 ("Who funds development and underutilised infrastructure?", f"Proposed split of ₹{tot_cr:.1f} crore: Suzuki R&D Center India and Maruti 40 percent (engineering, prototypes, test cells), public support 35 percent (the MNRE transport pilot scheme names 4-wheelers; ARAI runs its calls), an oil marketing company 15 percent (dispensing point), the fleet 10 percent. Station utilisation risk sits with the station partner under take-or-pay. None of this is committed."),
 ("What result would make you stop?", "No written regulator route by March 2027; backfire or NOx not controlled within 500 test-cell hours; delivered hydrogen still above ₹250/kg in 2030 with no credible path to ₹190/kg; or fleets refusing the power and boot loss in Phase 0 interviews."),
 ("What can realistically be achieved by 2030?", "An approved retrofit route, 30 cars with 18 months of independent data, a shared hydrogen dispensing point in Faridabad, supplier quotations for car-size cylinders, and a decision. Conditional expansion to 1,000 to 1,500 cars at up to 8 hub stations is possible only after gate 4; Round 1's 1,00,000 cars is not."),
 ("Why not a fuel-cell conversion for better efficiency?", "It halves hydrogen use per km but replaces the entire powertrain (stack, battery, motor, inverter, cooling, tank, controls) at ₹15 to 25 lakh per car in a pilot, more than a new BEV. It is not a retrofit in cost terms and has no retrofit approval route either."),
 ("Why keep the HCNG step if it displaces only 6 percent of energy?", "Because it is legal today (GSR 585(E), IS 17314), needs no vehicle change, and lowers CO and HC; it is an air-quality measure that lets CGD companies start blending. We no longer present it as decarbonisation."),
 ("How do you know fleets want this?", "We do not yet. Customer demand is a stated hypothesis; Phase 0 funds 30 structured interviews and telematics on 20 donor cars, and gate 1 requires evidence from at least three fleets before any engineering spend."),
 ("Is Suzuki endorsing this?", "No. Suzuki R&D Center India is proposed as engineering lead because of its CNG engine and calibration know-how and Suzuki's own hydrogen combustion prototypes (Burgman, Swift with AVL). No endorsement or partnership is claimed."),
]

